args/cmd x the reference guard (integ/refsem-a; owner ruling "args/cmd x
reference guard, Option 2, 2026-11-01": the guard stays frozen). Originals
from tests/runtime2/args_cmd/args_cmd.py:
  args_push_after_output.f  `print("e")`, then `args.push("z")`: legacy prints
  args_sort.f               `e` and fails with "cannot mutate const array:
                            args" (args.sort(): the same error). The VM refuses
                            before any effect as reference-guard:unresolved, a
                            misleading classification (Phase B would not change
                            the meaning: `args` is a const array in every
                            model). A temporary diagnostic-classification
                            difference; correcting the diagnostic is a
                            pre-release obligation.
  args_session_loop.session `nift -i -e '...; for(a : args) {...}' a`, piped:
                            refused (reference-guard:shared-part: a later
                            statement is assumed able to mutate a session
                            array). A temporary Phase A over-refusal; Phase B
                            must admit it.
  args_copy_push.f          `b := args; b.push("q")`: legacy aliases `args`
                            and grows it (2, 2). Phase B must establish the
                            final reference-and-immutability contract: aliasing
                            must not bypass a genuinely immutable binding, and
                            a successful mutation is NOT assumed to be the
                            right answer. Phase B rules on it; it does not
                            just admit it.

  args_push_after_output.f and args_sort.f are also the witnesses of the
  SUSPENDED args/cmd mutant 5 (tests/runtime2/args_cmd/mutants.sh: the script
  bindings made writable; equivalent while the guard refuses every
  script-unit mutation of `args`). Phase B must reinstate mutant 5; these
  cases must then reach the const-mutation error.
